0s Opel was riding a wave of success  in 1935 they became the first German  manufacturer to build over 100,000 cars  per year that made Opel the largest  maker of passenger cars in Europe  however this impressive growth was only  possible thanks to hefty cash injections  from American parent company General  Motors which took a majority stake in  oval in 1929 during the Great Depression  when the German economy started to pick  up the middle class developed and  interesting cars  during this time Opel began using  progressive American production methods  like assembly lines and modular  construction systems in 1935 Opel  started producing Germany's first mass  production car with a monocoque body the  Opel Olympia  [Music]  a slightly more compact version of the  Olympia's body was used to create the  cadet  it's simple technology was largely taken  from opals entry-level model the p4  [Music]  first off says a cadet is a military  trainee who often hopes to become an  officer but is still far from reaching  that goal and he thinks that's an apt  description of the oval cadet while it  had the monocoque body modeled on the  Olympia it lacks some of that much more  expensive cars finesse still at its  premiere in Berlin in 1936 Opel sales  director touted the cadet as an everyday  car for discerning customers and the  cadet was a hit soon the compact model  from rüsselsheim had become the  best-selling car in its class and the  quickest though it eat just 23  horsepower out of its 1.1 litre engine  it still left the competition in the  dust thanks to its low curb weight of  just 757 kilos  [Music]  neither man could do it with a little  patience and a heavy foot Christophe can  almost push the cadet to a hundred  kilometers an hour mark - stop it the  braking system features hydraulic drum  brakes on all four wheels  the base model cadet has two rigid axles  on leaf springs making for a bumpy ride  on country roads however this special  variant has an independent front  suspension it's tuned quite softly  though so when he rounds curves fast the  car really tilts  with my snow christophe says that like  most 30s era vehicles the opel cadet has  that streamlined shape and rounded  aerodynamic design that was so popular  at the time back then integrated  headlights were a real novelty before  that automakers had simply stuck lights  onto the body opals ad executives sang  the praises of the cars design saying  the front end keeps the cabin air tight  while driving and it's smooth rounded  shape makes it easy to clean good to  know  a stylized Zippo and adorns the cadets  hood it was only in the 1950s and 60s  that the airship was gradually replaced  by the lightning bolt logo Opel still  uses today its semaphore type turn  signals instead of blinkers and the  externally mounted spare tire  distinguished the cadet as a pre-war  model  [Music]  the interior is very Spartan with just a  few instruments and a lot of bakelite  like other cars of its day the trunk  space is only accessible from the inside  not very practical  when it came to the engine Opel employed  low-cost tried and true technology  Christos says the engine is a pretty  unspectacular cast-iron model with a 1.1  liters of cubic capacity and 23  horsepower that in powered vehicle since  1931 what's innovative was the cars  monocoque construction in which the body  shell and the undercarriage are melted  to create a single unit this  construction method saved weights making  the car faster and more fuel efficient  that also reduces production costs while  heightening safety because along the way  Opel's engineers had invented the  crumple zone  the crumple zone was a byproduct of the  cars unibody construction though it was  only patented at the end of the 1950's  by Mercedes engineer Bela bovania still  that oversight didn't hurt the cadet the  little Opel found over 30,000 buyers in  its first year alone  [Music]  Christophe says it's progressive  technology soon made that cadet the  best-selling car in its class in Germany  with a market share of 59 percent  ferdinand porsche even used it as a  comparison while developing the VW  beetle germany's first true people's car  in spite of its success the cadet didn't  earn that distinction while it was a  relatively low cost car at the time for  the masses it was still an unaffordable  luxury bus for the paul hop the middle  of overseas that's why over 30 percent  of the production was sold abroad cadets  could be found on the roads in South  Africa Argentina China and New Zealand  after world war ii broke out production  ceased the last original model cadet  rolled off the assembly line in may 1940  [Music]  dans Christophe tells us that while  production of the Opel Olympia resumed  in the post-war years the cadet wasn't  resurrected that's because as part of  the war reparations the entire  production line in rüsselsheim was  confiscated and shipped off to Russia  there the cadet was reborn as the Moscow  vich  400 and remained in production until  1959 thanks to its innovative monocoque  body the oval cadet remained competitive  for many years which Christophe says  qualifies it as a milestone and  automotive history  Ultima babysittin more than 107 thousand  rolled off the assembly line in four  years making the cadet Germany's  best-selling compact car of the pre-war  era in 1962 the model was reintroduced  with a snappy slogan okay like opel  cadet  [Music]  a total of six generations of the cadet  were produced until opal ceased  production in the early 1990s  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]

